the rattling noise of sewing machines  has become the soundtrack of Alice's  childhood she works 12 hours a day  Monday to Friday in this clothing  workshop in Istanbul the women here are  sewing underwear and Aras has to make  sure they never run out of fabric  she earns about 50 cents an hour of  course I'd rather go to school but it's  just not possible we have to pay a lot  of rent and food the water bill  everything is expensive that's why I  need to work and help my family  Alice is 11 years old and not the only  child in this workshop Musa has been  working here for two years like others  he is from Syria  Khalil from Damascus is 13 he is the  oldest among the kids the youngest here  is only 6 years old  the Turkish owner lets us film but only  because we promised we would not show  him in our report child labor is illegal  in Turkey  the owner knows this but says the  families would be much worse off without  the work these children might be safe  from the war and terror back home in  Syria but what they really need now is  education and a chance to build a future  despite efforts by the Turkish  government to get refugee children off  the streets and into public schools many  here remain out of the classroom many  other children are sent out by the  parents to beg on the streets they can  be seen everywhere in Istanbul up to 1  million Syrian refugees live in the city  about one-third of them are children no  one knows exactly how many of them are  working instead of going to school Alice  has finished work for today she takes me  home with her to meet her family she has  two little sisters her mother is  pregnant and had to stop working they  don't receive any financial support from  the Turkish state her father found a job  but even with the money Alice earns they  can still only barely make ends meet we  have to send her to work we don't have  any other choice every morning she cries  and tells me I want to go to school it's  heartbreaking I know she is too young to  work and many people would not offer her  a job because she's still a child but I  know the owner of that clothing workshop  and he said he wants to help us in Syria  I was the only one working and it was  enough to support my family even my  parents but here it's different  rent the kids expenses I just can't do  it all on my own I meet Akram Imam Ola  one of his stumbles district mayors he  admits that child labour is a problem in  Turkey one that has grown with the  arrival of the Syrian refugees our  options are limited do we try to get  these children into our schools give  them books and pens and school bags of  course we do get in your alarma but we  don't always reach all of them it's  often the case that more help is needed  then we are able to give that's why  others are trying to help in Alice's  neighborhood Syrian teachers are giving  classes even on weekends for children  who otherwise wouldn't be able to attend  school  Turkish Arabic or math the basics at  least for eros these are precious  moments when the sewing machines stop  rattling and she can be a child again if  only for a few hours
 

